\section{Maximum grade, latest date with surveys, the \texttt{maxgradesurvey}
module}\label{maxgradesurvey}

This module used to be \cref{disjunctmax} with mandatory surveys added:
graded assignments combined by disjunctive maximum, and every survey had to
be completed or the grade was F.
Those rules now live in [[disjunctmax]] itself
(\cref{disjunctmax-surveys}), since every summary module handles surveys
(\cref{surveys}).
A survey variant beside the plain module would only invite choosing the
plain one for a group that holds a survey, and then a survey's score, which
says nothing about the student, would enter the maximum.

The module stays, because scripts name it in
[[canvaslms results -S canvaslms.grades.maxgradesurvey]] and may import from
it.
It is now an alias that re-exports everything from [[disjunctmax]]---including
[[is_survey]] and [[survey_completed]], which this module used to define and
[[disjunctmax]] now imports from [[surveys]]---so those names keep working
here and are the very same objects.
Importing it emits a [[DeprecationWarning]], hidden by default for the reason
given in \cref{conjunctavgsurvey}.
<<[[maxgradesurvey.py]]>>=
"""
Deprecated alias of `canvaslms.grades.disjunctmax`, which handles surveys.

Use `canvaslms.grades.disjunctmax` instead: it takes the maximum over the
graded assignments and requires every survey (a classic Canvas quiz of
survey type) to be completed, see `canvaslms.grades.surveys`. This module
re-exports everything from `canvaslms.grades.disjunctmax`.
"""

import warnings

from canvaslms.grades.disjunctmax import *

<<helper functions>>

warnings.warn(
  "canvaslms.grades.maxgradesurvey is deprecated, "
  "use canvaslms.grades.disjunctmax, which handles surveys",
  DeprecationWarning, stacklevel=2)
@

We define the test file structure early, but place each test after the
functionality it verifies.
The import silences the deprecation warning, which one test checks
explicitly, so that it does not clutter every test run.
<<test [[maxgradesurvey.py]]>>=
"""Tests for canvaslms.grades.maxgradesurvey, an alias of disjunctmax."""
import importlib
import warnings

import pytest

import canvaslms.grades.disjunctmax as disjunctmax
with warnings.catch_warnings():
  warnings.simplefilter("ignore", DeprecationWarning)
  import canvaslms.grades.maxgradesurvey as maxgradesurvey
import canvaslms.grades.surveys as surveys

<<test functions>>
@

The public functions are the same objects as in [[disjunctmax]], and the
survey predicates are the shared ones.
<<test functions>>=
def test_alias_exports_disjunctmax_functions():
  for name in ["summarize_group", "summarize", "missing_assignments",
               "grade_max", "grade_to_int", "int_to_grade",
               "normalize_grade"]:
    assert getattr(maxgradesurvey, name) is getattr(disjunctmax, name)
  assert maxgradesurvey.is_survey is surveys.is_survey
  assert maxgradesurvey.survey_completed is surveys.survey_completed


def test_alias_warns_about_deprecation():
  with pytest.warns(DeprecationWarning, match="canvaslms.grades.disjunctmax"):
    importlib.reload(maxgradesurvey)
@

Same objects should give the same results; we check that on two exam
attempts and a survey, completed and not.
<<test functions>>=
def test_alias_gives_same_results(grading_objects):
  passed = grading_objects.graded(grading_objects.submission(
    "C", submitted_at="2026-05-01T10:00:00Z"), name="Exam 1")
  skipped = grading_objects.graded(None, name="Exam 2")
  done = grading_objects.survey(grading_objects.submission(
    "50", submitted_at="2026-05-02T10:00:00Z"))
  undone = grading_objects.survey(None, name="Second survey")
  users = [grading_objects.user()]

  for group in [[passed, skipped, done], [passed, skipped, done, undone]]:
    assert list(maxgradesurvey.summarize_group(group, users)) \
      == list(disjunctmax.summarize_group(group, users))
    assert list(maxgradesurvey.missing_assignments(group, users)) \
      == list(disjunctmax.missing_assignments(group, users))
@


\subsection{What changed for groups summarized with this module}

The alias is not a perfect stand-in for the old module, for two reasons.

\paragraph{What counts as a survey}
The old module took every assignment that is not graded with letters for a
survey (its [[grading_type]] was neither [[letter_grade]] nor
[[gpa_scale]]).
A FeedbackFruits item graded out of 100 points was thus mandatory and
contributed an F to the maximum.
Now it is graded work: one attempt among several that need not all be taken,
whose grade must be one [[disjunctmax]] can rank.
A numeric grade cannot be ranked, so such an assignment now raises an error
that names the grade and the assignment, where the old module quietly ranked
it as F.
The same goes for a New Quiz graded in points, since Canvas does not tell us
whether a New Quiz is a survey.

Let's pin the case that differs without an error: an A on the exam next to a
points-graded item the student never submitted.
The old module took the item for a survey and vetoed the A.
<<test functions>>=
def test_unsubmitted_points_assignment_does_not_veto(grading_objects):
  user = grading_objects.user()
  exam = grading_objects.graded(grading_objects.submission(
    "A", submitted_at="2026-05-01T10:00:00Z"), name="Exam")
  peer_review = grading_objects.points(None)

  grade, _, _ = maxgradesurvey.summarize(user, [exam, peer_review])

  assert grade == "A"
@

\paragraph{The grade vocabulary}
The old module read grades through its own [[normalize_survey_grade]], which
the alias no longer uses: [[disjunctmax]] reads them through the shared
vocabulary of \cref{gradealiases}.
For the grades a teacher sets, the two differ in three ways.
\enquote{complete} is now a passed attempt, where the old module ranked it as
F---a precaution against surveys that record completion, which the survey
rule now covers properly.
[[Fx]] now keeps its rank between F and P, so a student whose best attempt is
an Fx gets Fx rather than F.
And an unknown grade is an error rather than an F, as described above.

We keep [[normalize_survey_grade]] so that code importing it from here keeps
working, unchanged and with its tests.
Its contract was set membership against the grades the maximum can rank,
with [[P+]] normalized to [[P]] first; anything else, including
\enquote{complete}, became F.
The constant [[LETTER_GRADING_TYPES]], which held the old survey test, is
gone: keeping it would suggest that the grading type still decides what a
survey is.
<<helper functions>>=
RECOGNIZED_GRADES = {"A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "P", "F"}

def normalize_survey_grade(grade):
  """Normalize a Canvas grade the way the old maxgradesurvey did.

  P+ counts as P. Any grade not in RECOGNIZED_GRADES (e.g. None or a
  numeric survey score) is treated as F. Kept for importers only; the
  summary uses `disjunctmax`'s grade handling.
  """
  if grade == "P+":
    return "P"
  if grade in RECOGNIZED_GRADES:
    return grade
  return "F"
@

The kept function behaves as it always did: P+ passes, recognized grades pass
through untouched, and everything else---including the substring-test false
positives of an even older version---becomes F.
<<test functions>>=
def test_p_plus_normalizes_to_p():
  assert maxgradesurvey.normalize_survey_grade("P+") == "P"


def test_recognized_grades_pass_through():
  for grade in ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "P", "F"]:
    assert maxgradesurvey.normalize_survey_grade(grade) == grade


def test_unrecognized_grades_become_f():
  for grade in [None, "", "EP", "17.0", "complete"]:
    assert maxgradesurvey.normalize_survey_grade(grade) == "F"
@
